{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Emotion City Map\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Transforming Urban Emotions into Data-Driven Spatial Systems\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Student: Kaiying Huang\line Project theme: An emotional map of the city\line Core idea: park = relaxed/open, commercial = stressful/dense, transport = tense/fast\line Software environments: Python, Cinema 4D, Processing\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li0 \fi0 {\pict\pngblip\picw1680\pich960\picwgoal10080\pichgoal5760 }\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li0 \fi0 OSM category counts\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Project Status\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Goal\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This project transforms machine-learning outputs, including vector embeddings, clusters, and emotional parameters, into design-driving systems for spatial generation. The project asks how a city can be read as an emotional field. Parks are interpreted as relaxed and open, commercial areas as stressful and dense, and transport nodes as tense and fast.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The final design outcome is an emotional city generator. Each emotional condition becomes a spatial rule:\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Relaxed / open: lower height, wider spacing, slower movement, blue-green tone.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Stressful / dense: taller height, tighter spacing, faster movement, red-orange tone.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Tense / fast: directional movement, transitional rhythm, compressed corridors.\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The workflow links data collection, vectorisation, machine learning, visualisation, and 3D generation. The data does not remain as analysis only. It is translated into 32 architectural fragments and animated as a one-minute spatial system.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Part 1: Projecting Between Domains\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 1.1 Web Scraping and Data Collection\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The project uses three main datasets from two different domains: Pinterest image data and OpenStreetMap urban point data. A Google Maps review collector is also prepared as an optional extension that can be run with an API key.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Dataset 1: Park / Relaxed Image Dataset\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Source: Pinterest\line Domain: image / social media image search\line Folder: {\f1 photos1/}\line Metadata: {\f1 metadata/pinterest_future_city_metadata.csv}\line Collected elements: 200 image files\line Readable images used in analysis: 188\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This dataset represents relaxed and open urban atmospheres. It contains park, garden, landscape, and green-space references. In the design logic, these images support the low-density, slow-moving, open spatial condition.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Dataset 2: Commercial / Stressful Image Dataset\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Source: Pinterest\line Domain: image / social media image search\line Folder: {\f1 photos2/}\line Metadata: {\f1 metadata/pinterest_business environment_metadata.csv}\line Collected elements: 200 image files\line Readable images used in analysis: 199\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This dataset represents dense commercial atmospheres. It includes business environments, shopping areas, office-like environments, and visually busier urban references. In the design logic, these images support the high-density, compressed, vertical, and fast-moving condition.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Dataset 3: OpenStreetMap Urban POI Dataset\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Source: OpenStreetMap / Overpass API\line Domain: live spatial / urban point data\line Output: {\f1 workflow/01_data_collection/dataset4_openstreetmap_poi/outputs/openstreetmap_poi_dataset.csv}\line Collected elements: 500\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Category distribution:\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\f1 park relaxed}: 280\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\f1 commercial stressful}: 220\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The OpenStreetMap dataset contains real urban elements with names, tags, coordinates, and category labels. It gives the project a spatial anchor: instead of only using atmosphere images, the system also works with actual city locations such as parks, gardens, shops, and offices.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Google Maps Review Extension:\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Google Maps review collector is located at:\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\f1 workflow/01_data_collection/dataset3_google_maps_reviews/}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 It is prepared to collect 200-300 real review records using the Google Places API, but it requires {\f1 GOOGLE_MAPS_API_KEY}. Since no key was available in this environment, the review dataset is documented as a prepared extension rather than used as a generated result.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 1.2 Dataset Vectorisation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Vectorisation was used to translate both image and text/spatial datasets into machine-readable feature spaces.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Text / POI Vectorisation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Input dataset: OpenStreetMap POI names and tags\line Output: {\f1 workflow/02_vectorisation/outputs/text_vectorisation_tfidf_vs_hashing.csv}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Two vectorisation methods were compared:\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 1.\tx360\tab TF-IDF + SVD\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 2.\tx360\tab HashingVectorizer + SVD\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 TF-IDF was selected because it is interpretable. It allows the report to connect words and OSM tags such as {\f1 park}, {\f1 garden}, {\f1 shop}, and {\f1 office} back to design decisions. This is useful for a design workflow because the features are not a black box.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 HashingVectorizer was selected as a comparison because it is faster and more scalable. It does not store a vocabulary, so it is less readable, but it can process larger streams of text with a fixed feature size.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Result:\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab TF-IDF silhouette: 0.235\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Hashing silhouette: 0.109\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Cluster agreement between both methods: ARI 0.490\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Interpretation:\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 TF-IDF performed better for this dataset. OSM tags are short and structured, so the vocabulary itself is meaningful. Hashing was faster but less interpretable and produced weaker separation.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li0 \fi0 {\pict\pngblip\picw1440\pich1200\picwgoal8640\pichgoal7200 }\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li0 \fi0 TF-IDF clusters\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li0 \fi0 {\pict\pngblip\picw1440\pich1200\picwgoal8640\pichgoal7200 }\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li0 \fi0 Hashing clusters\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Image Vectorisation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Input datasets: Pinterest park images and Pinterest business images\line Output: {\f1 workflow/02_vectorisation/outputs/image_vectorisation_colour_texture.csv}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Two image vectorisation strategies were compared:\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 1.\tx360\tab Colour statistics and HSV histograms\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 2.\tx360\tab Colour + texture / edge features\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Colour features were selected because the emotional contrast between park and commercial atmospheres is partly visual: parks tend to contain green-blue, softer, and more open images; commercial environments tend to contain stronger contrast, hard surfaces, signage, glass, and artificial lighting.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Texture and edge features were added as a comparison because commercial spaces often have more hard boundaries, visual clutter, and sharper geometry.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Result:\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Valid images: 387\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Colour feature silhouette: 0.306\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Colour + texture feature silhouette: 0.251\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Cluster agreement: ARI 0.822\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Interpretation:\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Colour separated the datasets more strongly than texture. Texture added detail, but also noise, because some park images include paths, buildings, and hard edges, while some commercial images have smooth surfaces.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li0 \fi0 {\pict\pngblip\picw1440\pich1200\picwgoal8640\pichgoal7200 }\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li0 \fi0 Image PCA\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 1.3 API Interaction for Structured Data Retrieval\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The API interaction component is prepared in:\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\f1 workflow/01_data_collection/dataset5_api_synthetic_emotion_text/scripts/generate_emotion_text_api.py}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This script uses the OpenAI API to generate structured emotional city observations. Each generated item includes:\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab sensory observation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab emotional interpretation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab spatial rule\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab keywords\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The script is designed to augment the scraped and spatial datasets. It creates text that directly connects emotion to spatial grammar, for example:\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab relaxed park atmosphere -> horizontal expansion, wide spacing, slow rhythm\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab stressful commercial atmosphere -> vertical pressure, dense grid, fast rhythm\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab tense transport atmosphere -> directional corridors and compressed waiting\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This part requires the user\u8217's own {\f1 OPENAI_API_KEY}. The script is included and ready, but no API key is stored in the project.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 1.4 Machine Learning\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Two machine-learning models were trained and compared using the OSM POI dataset and vectorised features.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Input:\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\f1 workflow/03_machine_learning/outputs/osm_ml_clusters_kmeans_vs_gmm.csv}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Feature columns:\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab value1\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab value2\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab TF-IDF SVD x/y\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Hashing SVD x/y\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Model 1: KMeans Clustering\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 KMeans was used to group urban elements into emotion-space clusters. It is suitable for this project because the design system needs clear cluster labels that can be mapped into spatial rules.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Result:\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab KMeans silhouette: 0.635\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Model 2: Gaussian Mixture Model\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Gaussian Mixture Model was used as a softer clustering comparison. Unlike KMeans, GMM assumes data can belong to probabilistic distributions. This is conceptually useful for emotional ambiguity, where a place might be partly calm and partly stressful.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Result:\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab GMM silhouette: 0.494\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Comparison:\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 KMeans performed better in this dataset because the emotional parameters were clearly separated: relaxed/open elements have low {\f1 value1/value2}, while commercial/stressful elements have high {\f1 value1/value2}. GMM is still valuable as a design idea because it supports transitional emotional zones, but KMeans produced cleaner clusters for direct fragment generation.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li0 \fi0 {\pict\pngblip\picw1680\pich1200\picwgoal10080\pichgoal7200 }\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li0 \fi0 KMeans emotion space\par}
