{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Part 2: Design Tool Integration\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 2.1 Project Brief\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The design tool workflow uses Python, Cinema 4D, and Processing.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Python is used for data processing, vectorisation, machine learning, and fragment parameter generation. It translates the collected datasets into a CSV of spatial rules.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Cinema 4D is used as the main 3D modelling and animation environment. It reads the fragment parameter CSV and generates 32 architectural fragments with different heights, colours, spacing logic, and animation keyframes.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Processing is used as a real-time animation and interaction environment. It tests the emotional city logic through mouse-controlled emotion and density. Moving the mouse left produces a relaxed/open park city; moving it right produces a dense/stressful commercial city.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 2.2 Integrated Workflows\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Workflow A: Data to Fragment Parameters\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Pinterest images and OSM data are processed in Python. Image colour/texture features and OSM text vectors are clustered. The cluster outputs are translated into height, spacing, distortion, movement speed, colour, and scene position.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Output:\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\f1 workflow/05_design_tool_integration/fragment_parameters/emotion_city_fragments_32.csv}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Workflow B: Fragment Parameters to Cinema 4D\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Cinema 4D reads the fragment CSV and generates a 3D scene. Each fragment stores:\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab source urban element\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab emotional category\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab KMeans cluster\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab GMM cluster\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab height, width, depth\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab distortion\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab movement speed\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab RGB colour\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab scene position\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Cinema 4D script:\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\f1 workflow/05_design_tool_integration/cinema4d/generate_emotion_city_c4d.py}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Workflow C: Emotion Rules to Processing\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Processing uses the same emotional rules in an interactive sketch. The user controls the emotional axis with the mouse:\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab left = relaxed, open, park-like\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab right = stressful, dense, commercial-like\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Processing sketch:\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\f1 python_processing/python_processing.pde}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This sketch demonstrates the rule system live: height, spacing, distortion, colour, pulse, and density all change continuously along the emotional axis.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 2.3 Iterative Reconfiguration\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The final fragment system contains 32 architectural fragments:\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\f1 workflow/05_design_tool_integration/fragment_parameters/emotion_city_fragments_32.csv}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Each fragment is generated through rule-based transformation. The fragments are not manually modelled one by one. They are generated from machine-learning cluster outputs and emotional design parameters.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Design rules:\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab higher {\f1 value1} -> greater stress -> taller fragments\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab higher {\f1 value2} -> greater density -> tighter spacing and larger mass\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab KMeans cluster -> fragment variation group\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab emotion category -> colour palette\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab movement speed -> animation rhythm\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab distortion -> spatial instability\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The result is a systematic family of architectural fragments rather than isolated objects.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 2.4 3D Scenes and Animation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Cinema 4D animation:\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\f1 workflow/05_design_tool_integration/cinema4d/generate_emotion_city_c4d.py}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This script sets a 1800-frame animation at 30 fps, which equals 60 seconds. It creates a scene with a ground plane, lights, camera movement, and 32 animated fragments.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Processing animation:\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\f1 python_processing/python_processing.pde}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This sketch creates a real-time emotional city where the viewer can move between relaxed/open and stressful/dense urban conditions.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Preview animation:\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\f1 workflow/06_report_assets/emotion_city_preview_65s.mp4}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This preview is 65 seconds long and was generated from the fragment CSV. It can be used as a quick submission/crit preview while a higher-quality Cinema 4D render is prepared.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Project Conclusion\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This project converts urban media and spatial datasets into an emotional generation system. Images provide atmosphere, OSM data provides real urban locations, vectorisation translates the datasets into feature spaces, machine learning extracts cluster structures, and the design tools transform those clusters into architectural fragments.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The project demonstrates a continuous workflow:\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 data collection -> vectorisation -> machine learning -> visual analysis -> fragment rules -> Cinema 4D / Processing animation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The emotional city is therefore not only a visual metaphor. It is a design tool where data-driven emotional categories directly produce spatial variation.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
